---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Getting started — Scenario Practice"
  description="How to open a scenario in Codespaces or locally, run the tests, and read your feedback."
>
  <div class="prose">
    <h1>Getting started</h1>

    <h2>Accept the scenario</h2>
    <p>
      Open a scenario page and press <strong>Start this scenario</strong>. GitHub
      creates your own copy of the repository under your account.
    </p>
    <p>
      Private or public is your call — grading works either way. A Grade run takes
      about a minute, so a private repo's free monthly Actions allowance is not a
      real limit. Public repos get unlimited Actions minutes, but anyone can read
      your work, including other trainees.
    </p>

    <h2>Option A — GitHub Codespaces</h2>
    <p>
      From your repository, choose <strong>Code → Codespaces → Create codespace</strong>.
      The environment installs the toolchain and dependencies for you. The
      scenario's README gives the one command that starts it.
    </p>
    <p>
      The database is created, migrated, and seeded on first run — no database
      setup, no Docker, no other services. The README shows how to explore the
      running API.
    </p>
    <p>
      Codespaces has a monthly free allowance on personal accounts. If you run
      out, everything below works the same on your own machine.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>If the editor fails to connect.</strong> Codespaces occasionally
      fails to start its editor server, showing "The workbench failed to connect
      to the server". Stop the codespace — <strong>Code → Codespaces → … → Stop
      codespace</strong> — and open it again; a cold start clears it. If it
      happens twice, switch to Option B and work locally. Nothing about your
      score depends on which one you use.
    </p>

    <h2>Option B — your own machine</h2>
    <p>
      Clone your repository and run the one command in the scenario's README.
      Each scenario page lists the stack it targets; the only prerequisite is
      the toolchain you already use for that stack every day. No database setup,
      no Docker, no other services.
    </p>

    <h2>Run the tests</h2>
    <p>
      The suite ships red for every work item and green for everything that
      already worked. Run it with your stack's usual test command — the README
      names it.
    </p>
    <p>
      To get the same per-item breakdown the grader produces, use the check
      script instead. Every scenario ships these two scripts, and they read the
      same work-item list the grader does, so the split always matches:
    </p>
    <pre><code># macOS / Linux
./scripts/check.sh

# Windows
./scripts/check.ps1</code></pre>
    <p>
      A summary prints one line per work item. Your goal is to turn each
      <code>FAIL</code> into a <code>PASS</code> while
      <code>Regression</code> stays green. <code>NO TESTS</code> usually means
      the work item's tests aren't being discovered — if you renamed or skipped
      tests, restore them; if the repo is untouched, it's a scenario problem
      worth reporting. <code>SKIPPED</code> means the category contains skipped
      tests (<code>Skip = "…"</code> / <code>@Disabled</code>) and scores zero
      until every test in it runs.
    </p>

    <h2>Get your feedback</h2>
    <p>
      Push to your repository. A workflow runs the same checks and reports a score
      per work item on the Actions tab. Commit as often as you like — every push
      is graded, and the last one counts.
    </p>

    <h2>How you are judged beyond the tests</h2>
    <p>
      Tests score correctness. A reviewer also looks
      at whether your work fits the codebase: business logic in the service layer,
      DTOs at the boundary, validation on new endpoints, tests of your own, and a
      diff without leftovers. Each scenario ships that rubric in
      <code>docs/rubric.md</code>. Read it before you start, not after.
    </p>

    <h2>Ground rules</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Use whatever AI assistance you like. It is not policed, and the rubric
        scores your diff, not how you produced it. You will still be asked to
        explain your changes.
      </li>
      <li>Do not edit the shipped tests to make them pass. Add your own instead.</li>
      <li>
        Breaking existing behaviour costs more points than leaving a story
        unfinished. When in doubt, protect what works.
      </li>
      <li>
        Follow the conventions already in the codebase, even where you would have
        chosen differently. Fitting in is part of the exercise.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
